-- Passage rg6-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5ae99b02-78eb-5b11-a544-e79e00837ff3', 'rg6-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Walking School Bus$q$, $q$Three years ago, Nadia Haddad stood at her front window and counted cars. Between seven forty-five and eight o'clock, more than sixty vehicles crept past her house toward Fernwood Elementary School. Most of them carried just one child. Nadia lived only half a mile from the school, and so did many of those families.

Nadia, a retired bus driver, had an idea. What if children walked to school together in a group, the way they would ride together on a bus? An adult would lead the group along a set route. The "bus" would stop at certain corners at certain times, and children would join as it passed.

She started small. The first walking school bus had four children and one adult: Nadia herself. Parents were nervous at first, so Nadia printed a map of the route and a schedule of every stop. She also asked the school to post a crossing guard at the busiest intersection.

Word spread quickly. By the end of the first month, eleven children were walking with her. Two parents offered to lead a second route on the other side of the neighborhood. Teachers noticed that the walkers arrived calm and ready to learn, and the principal reported that the morning line of cars had grown shorter.

Today Fernwood has five walking routes and more than forty regular walkers. Other schools in the district have asked Nadia to explain how she began. Her answer is always the same. A big change, she tells them, can start with one person counting cars at a window.$q$, 'draft')
