-- Passage rg6-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2879c995-c93d-5b27-8971-dc94398eaacd', 'rg6-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$When Ice Was Shipped Across the Sea$q$, $q$Two hundred years ago, keeping food cold in summer was nearly impossible for most families. Meat spoiled, milk turned sour, and fresh fruit lasted only a few days. Then a new business changed the way people lived: the ice trade.

The idea was simple. Many northern lakes froze solid every winter. Workers could cut that ice into large blocks, store it, and sell it months later in warmer places. At first, many people laughed at the plan. They believed the ice would melt long before it reached a buyer.

The early ice sellers solved this problem by studying insulation. They packed the blocks tightly together in thick-walled icehouses and covered them with sawdust. The sawdust trapped cool air and slowed the melting. Ice stored this way could last through an entire summer. Ships lined with sawdust carried ice to cities far to the south.

As ice became cheaper, daily life began to shift. Families bought wooden iceboxes, which were cabinets with a block of ice in the top section. Butchers could keep meat fresh for longer. Farmers could ship milk to towns that were farther away. Cold drinks, once a treat for the wealthy, became common on hot afternoons.

The winter harvest also created jobs. Each January, crews marked the frozen lakes in grids, sawed out the blocks, and floated them to shore along cut channels. Whole towns depended on this work during the coldest months.

The ice trade lasted about one hundred years. Eventually, ice-making machines and electric refrigerators replaced it. Still, the ice trade taught people to expect cold food at any time of year, and that expectation helped the new machines succeed.$q$, 'draft')
