-- Passage rg6-connections-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6697c892-f4cc-5799-b051-f29259a92458', 'rg6-connections-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Rowing as One$q$, $q$In most races, the fastest athlete wins. In rowing, a boat with eight strong rowers can lose to a boat with eight weaker ones. The reason is a single idea that every rower must learn: timing matters more than strength.

A racing boat is long and narrow, and it sits low in the water. Each rower pulls one oar. If one rower's oar enters the water a moment too early, it acts like a brake. The boat tips slightly to one side and slows down. Even a tiny wobble wastes the energy of everyone on board.

To stay together, rowers depend on two people. The first is the stroke, the rower who sits nearest the back of the boat. Everyone else watches the stroke and copies that rower's motion exactly. The second is the coxswain, a small, light person who steers and calls out commands. The coxswain does not row at all. Instead, the coxswain keeps the rhythm steady and tells the crew when to push harder.

Coaches train timing in creative ways. Some crews practice with their eyes closed so they learn to feel the boat's rhythm. Others row while the coach claps a steady beat from a nearby motorboat.

Teams that master timing report an unusual feeling. The boat seems to lift and glide, and the work feels lighter. Rowers call this feeling swing. When a crew finds swing, eight separate athletes move like a single machine, and that is when races are won.$q$, 'draft')
