   $q$How does the passage connect the safety brake to the rise of skyscrapers?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The safety brake made steel frames stronger, so buildings could hold more floors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"The brake made stairs safer to climb, so people agreed to walk to higher floors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-detail"},{"text":"Skyscrapers were built first, and the brake was invented to serve their tall shafts.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-sequence"},{"text":"Safe elevators made top floors valuable, which gave builders a reason to build higher.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Safe elevators made top floors valuable, which gave builders a reason to build higher.$q$, $q$Paragraph 1 says builders "saw no reason to go higher" because of stairs, and paragraph 5 says safe elevators made top floors "the most valuable." Steel frames were a separate factor that worked together with the brake; the brake did not strengthen them.$q$, 'draft', '82913189-f6e1-542d-8308-b0bae4371df0')
