-- Passage rg6-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('abd3c31b-7e60-51cd-83cb-8c41abc222c2', 'rg6-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Our Streets Need More Trees$q$, $q$Last July, I stood at a bus stop on Harmon Avenue in the middle of the afternoon. There was no shade anywhere. The sidewalk was so hot that I could feel it through my sneakers. Two blocks away, on tree-lined Oak Street, the air felt noticeably cooler. That difference convinced me that our city should plant many more street trees.

The first reason is temperature. Trees shade pavement and buildings, and they release water from their leaves, which cools the air around them. Streets with full tree cover can be several degrees cooler on summer days than bare streets nearby. Cooler streets mean people can walk, wait for buses, and play outside more safely.

Trees also help during storms. Their roots soak up rainwater, and their leaves catch some rain before it hits the ground. This slows the flood of water rushing into storm drains. On Harmon Avenue, wide puddles often cover the crosswalks after heavy rain. More trees could reduce that problem.

Some people argue that trees cost too much. Young trees must be planted, watered, and trimmed, and their roots can crack sidewalks. These concerns are real. However, choosing the right kinds of trees and giving them wide planting spaces can prevent most damage. Over many years, the savings from lower cooling bills and fewer flooded streets can outweigh the cost of care.

This fall, the parks department is asking residents to suggest streets for new trees. I urge every reader to fill out the form and name Harmon Avenue. It deserves the same cool shade as Oak Street.$q$, 'draft')
